1
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:03,390
Who is it?

2
00:00:03,390 --> 00:00:05,260
Dad.

3
00:00:05,260 --> 00:00:09,670
Come on in, Dad.

4
00:00:09,670 --> 00:00:11,770
I thought you might be hungry.

5
00:00:11,770 --> 00:00:14,460
I brought you a chicken sandwich and a glass of milk.

6
00:00:14,460 --> 00:00:15,500
I am hungry.

7
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:18,440
Thanks, Dad.

8
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:22,480
What time is it, anyway?

9
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:25,820
Ten o'clock.

10
00:00:25,820 --> 00:00:27,360
What are you working on?

11
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:30,970
I'm writing a story for the high-school paper.

12
00:00:30,970 --> 00:00:32,420
Can't you finish it tomorrow?

13
00:00:32,420 --> 00:00:36,450
No, I have to turn it in in the morning.

14
00:00:36,450 --> 00:00:39,170
What's it about?

15
00:00:39,170 --> 00:00:43,460
I'm writing an article on the feelings about graduation.

16
00:00:43,460 --> 00:00:46,420
And ... how do you feel?

17
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:48,860
Me?

18
00:00:48,860 --> 00:00:50,540
A little scared.

19
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:53,970
And excited, too.

20
00:00:53,970 --> 00:00:55,680
I felt the same way.

21
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:58,860
The scary part's leaving home and moving to college.

22
00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:03,880
Oh, leaving home is part of growing up.

23
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:07,110
Well, don't work all night.

24
00:01:07,110 --> 00:01:07,950
I don't mind.

25
00:01:07,950 --> 00:01:09,850
I enjoy writing.

26
00:01:09,850 --> 00:01:10,540
Well,

27
00:01:10,540 --> 00:01:12,460
maybe you should think about becoming a writer.

28
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:14,710
Maybe I should.

29
00:01:14,710 --> 00:01:17,260
You have lots of time to decide.

30
00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:21,200
That's the worst part--making decisions.

31
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:22,890
You'll be OK.

32
00:01:22,890 --> 00:01:24,180
Good night, Son.

33
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:35,190
Good night, Dad.

34
00:01:35,190 --> 00:01:37,330
"Hail to the victors valiant,

35
00:01:37,330 --> 00:01:39,510
Hail to the conquering heroes,

36
00:01:39,510 --> 00:01:41,920
Hail, Hail to Michigan,

37
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:44,000
The champions of the West!"

38
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,500
Ah, good morning, Robbie.

39
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:48,670
Good morning, Dad.

40
00:01:48,670 --> 00:01:50,150
How's my grandson?

41
00:01:50,150 --> 00:01:52,210
Fine, Grandpa. Fine!

42
00:01:52,210 --> 00:01:53,960
What's all the cheering about?

43
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:56,770
Did the University of Michigan win another football game?

44
00:01:56,770 --> 00:01:58,400
Better than that.

45
00:01:58,400 --> 00:01:59,650
Tell him, Philip.

46
00:01:59,650 --> 00:02:02,090
I just spoke with Charley Rafer.

47
00:02:02,090 --> 00:02:04,110
Who's Charley Rafer?

48
00:02:04,110 --> 00:02:06,870
He's the Dean of Admissions for the University of Michigan.

49
00:02:06,870 --> 00:02:09,710
And it turns out he's a classmate of Philip's.

50
00:02:09,710 --> 00:02:12,060
We were both on the tennis team.

51
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:13,870
Great!

52
00:02:13,870 --> 00:02:15,270
It is great.

53
00:02:15,270 --> 00:02:16,760
He's going to be in New York tomorrow

54
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:19,200
to interview applicants for admission.

55
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:22,730
And he's agreed to fit you into his schedule.

56
00:02:22,730 --> 00:02:25,520
But I may not want to go to Michigan.

57
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:28,420
It's one of the best schools in the country, Robbie.

58
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:29,710
I studied medicine there.

59
00:02:29,710 --> 00:02:32,750
Your grandfather went to the Engineering School there.

60
00:02:32,750 --> 00:02:34,430
I know that, but ...

61
00:02:34,430 --> 00:02:37,190
You said you wanted to be a doctor like your father.

62
00:02:37,190 --> 00:02:39,030
Not exactly.

63
00:02:39,030 --> 00:02:42,120
You couldn't pick a finer medical school than Michigan.

64
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:43,990
Yes, I know that.

65
00:02:43,990 --> 00:02:47,190
Let's meet with Charley at the university club.

66
00:02:47,190 --> 00:02:49,520
Ten o'clock tomorrow morning.

67
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:52,630
It doesn't mean you're going to Michigan.

68
00:02:52,630 --> 00:02:54,860
It doesn't mean you have to be a doctor.

69
00:02:54,860 --> 00:02:58,350
But the interview will be good experience for you.

70
00:02:58,350 --> 00:03:01,910
In that case, it's OK.

71
00:03:01,910 --> 00:03:04,460
Dad, growing up means

72
00:03:04,460 --> 00:03:07,860
making my own decisions, doesn't it?

73
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:09,290
You're right, Robbie.

74
00:03:09,290 --> 00:03:13,340
But, like your Grandpa suggested,

75
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:15,650
have the interview.

76
00:03:15,650 --> 00:03:19,750
And then make your own decision.

77
00:03:19,750 --> 00:03:21,450
That sounds fine.

78
00:03:21,450 --> 00:03:22,650
I know it's sudden, Robbie,

79
00:03:22,650 --> 00:03:26,280
but this is an important opportunity.

80
00:03:26,280 --> 00:03:28,120
We'll head down there first thing tomorrow morning.

81
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:28,810
OK?

82
00:03:28,810 --> 00:03:33,090
Sure, Dad.

83
00:03:33,090 --> 00:03:37,280
I want you to know something, Son.

84
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:41,400
I'm ... very proud of you.

85
00:03:41,400 --> 00:03:45,020
Thanks, Dad.

86
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:47,270
Well, I've got an appointment at the hospital.

87
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:49,640
I'll see you all at dinnertime.

88
00:03:49,640 --> 00:03:52,850
Bye, Dad.

89
00:03:52,850 --> 00:03:58,350
Is something still wrong, Robbie?

90
00:03:58,350 --> 00:04:00,960
I'll be OK.

91
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:03,950
Going away to college for the first time

92
00:04:03,950 --> 00:04:08,880
always makes one a little nervous.

93
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:10,690
I guess so.

94
00:04:10,690 --> 00:04:13,690
I'll be OK. I just need time to think.
